[{:db/id "q-330783/field-1/c0aca680a9f9694ccc4d69161fb6c05b9f3f5fd43df0d5eb31190622891e2f0f" :answer/id #uuid "c29290c2-cef4-5dd9-ba64-579a673fc214" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{35}{32}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330783/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "1c93452e-d2d6-5e13-9bbb-3de17c87c1ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-330783/field-1/c0aca680a9f9694ccc4d69161fb6c05b9f3f5fd43df0d5eb31190622891e2f0f" :answer-field/choices ["q-330783/field-1/c0aca680a9f9694ccc4d69161fb6c05b9f3f5fd43df0d5eb31190622891e2f0f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-330783/field-2/8fecdf7f7728dd6feabc9d285697d15fc8769cff8b6565f49e1315830aa42290" :answer/id #uuid "6f5a7344-aeb5-56d9-b316-c49caec57822" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{67}{32}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330783/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a230cae-a71a-5aba-9dbe-0a57109e4599" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-330783/field-2/8fecdf7f7728dd6feabc9d285697d15fc8769cff8b6565f49e1315830aa42290" :answer-field/choices ["q-330783/field-2/8fecdf7f7728dd6feabc9d285697d15fc8769cff8b6565f49e1315830aa42290"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-330783" :question/id #uuid "38a50945-231c-563e-aeb6-1ed87af64dd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-330783" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x+y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) Method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $\\frac{29}{8}$ | {{field-1}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nAll four sample slopes are already computed. So, we just need to find the increment on $y.$\n\nWe compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot \\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{29}{8})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\frac{105}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $\\frac{29}{8}$ | $\\frac{35}{32}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{35}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{67}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $\\frac{29}{8}$ | $\\frac{35}{32}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{67}{32}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈\\frac{67}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-330783/field-1" "q-330783/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "caff9428-bce5-5aca-8e6d-3801a43b486e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-330783"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-331162/field-1/9984b308216601b412a0b5ae14b5a1648d394b9481ce3be5b110af1a3a3e2fc1" :answer/id #uuid "d6c1f013-b704-5050-984a-9c9fc5c74573" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{ }x+{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331162/field-1/b28db9c87e67a37cdd90ddaa5b594c6a500055d811daeaed07fb20d5cc699be2" :answer/id #uuid "5fe4f970-e891-50d7-8396-d0ab66c53315" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331162/field-1/695c84a6779fd8acb599dbd71f0fcb3ca3584e8e87c5a6e46dfa94757540782e" :answer/id #uuid "2281f205-bf76-5103-85cf-4455fe39df17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{ }x+Δx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331162/field-1/829f5373e3805b9727bb877f0ca3a791dab276f1958bec5a912f314008582db0" :answer/id #uuid "161446f8-3c75-5b10-8bdd-f7231a0786a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{ }x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331162/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2a5ffac5-bc6a-5d96-b900-5d2c46343726" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-331162/field-1/b28db9c87e67a37cdd90ddaa5b594c6a500055d811daeaed07fb20d5cc699be2" :answer-field/choices ["q-331162/field-1/9984b308216601b412a0b5ae14b5a1648d394b9481ce3be5b110af1a3a3e2fc1" "q-331162/field-1/b28db9c87e67a37cdd90ddaa5b594c6a500055d811daeaed07fb20d5cc699be2" "q-331162/field-1/695c84a